{"study_accession":"PRJNA682052","secondary_study_accession":"SRP295502","title":"Contribution of resident and circulating precursors to tumor-infiltrating CD8+ T cell populations in lung cancer [scRNA-Seq]","description":"Purpose: The goal of this study is to delineate the processes of CD8+ T cell differentiation in primary NSCLC by integrating transcriptomic and tcr profiles. Methods: A combination of single cell RNA and TCR sequencing (scRNA-seq and scTCR- seq) was used, in tumors, normal tissues adjacent to the tumor (juxta-tumor), and circulating blood derived from 11 patients with untreated, primary NSCLC. Results: We show that precursor, memory-like CD8+ TILs include 2 main populations: one is also present in the blood (circulating precursors); the other is also present in juxta-tumor tissue and bears markers of memory resident T cells (resident precursors). Both precursor subtypes differentiate into a main population of terminal effectors through a similar “transitional” stage. Terminal effectors are not observed in blood or juxta-tumor tissue, are more clonally expanded, and express signatures of exhaustion. A significant proportion of transitional and terminal effectors also express cell cycle signatures and Ki67, suggesting that clonal expansion occurs in situ is part of the terminal differentiation process. Overall design: 11 patients with early-stage, untreated NSCLC","pubmed":"33514641","geo_series":"","n_samples":17,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN16979871","study_accession":"PRJNA682052","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"study","title":"P60_Tumor","description":"P60_Tumor","sex":"male","geo_sample":"GSM4952964","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2021-01-30","attributes":[["source_name","NSCLC tumor tissue"],["tumor stage","pT2aN0"],["tissue","NSCLC tumor tissue"],["smoking status","smoker"],["tumor type","ADK"],["sex","male"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR13181494","experiment_accession":"SRX9619540","library_layout":"P